A-E005 | Llama Stack eval provider backed by TrustyAI execution path | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E004 | New platform evaluation service | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E005 | Llama Stack eval provider backed by new evaluation service | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P1 |\n\n## Dependency DAG (mermaid)\n\n## DAG Justification (table: edge, rule, rationale)\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n| HLR-1 | P0 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 (gated by E003) |\n| HLR-3 | P0 | E006 (schema from E002) |\n| HLR-4 | P1 | BRANCH-*-E004 |\n| HLR-5 | P1 | E007 |\n| HLR-6 | P1 | E008 |\n| HLR-7 | P1 | BRANCH-*-E005 |\n| HLR-8 | P2 | BRANCH-*-E004 (bundled: same run-record surface) |\n| HLR-9 | P2 | E009 |\n\n## Conditional Branches\n\nExplain E001 outcomes A and B.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- Missing strategy sections: Impacted Teams, Dependencies, Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then), NFRs — teams inferred from architecture context; ACs derived from HLRs.\n- Documentation epic team assumed (RHOAI Documentation) — no owning team stated anywhere.\n- Branch B maps new evaluation service to TrustyAI component (canonical list has no evaluation-service component) — if the service lands in a new repo, repo onboarding may be needed (noted in branch epic).\n- HLR-8 (P2) bundled into P0 execution epic — same run-record surface; splitting would duplicate the execution path.\n- Docs epic priority P0 via Critical→P0 mapping despite HLR-9 being P2 (per docs-authoring priority exception).\n- Benchmark licensing may require legal sign-off beyond the technical classification.\n\n## Triage — not needed (went through full decomposition).\n\nSkip triage section; maybe include a \"Staff Engineer Input\" note: none present.\n\nNow epic bodies. Each needs: Title line, Description, Scope, Acceptance Criteria, HLR Traceability. Plus extra sections where relevant (risks, assumptions, open questions). No sibling IDs in bodies.\n\n**E001 body:**\n\n# Investigation: Evaluation execution engine — extend LMEvalJob vs. new evaluation service\n\n## Description\nResolve the strategy's first open question... whether the platform's evaluation execution path builds on TrustyAI's existing LMEvalJob (Tech Preview, wraps lm-evaluation-harness) or a new evaluation service is needed that can also serve Llama Stack's eval provider API. Deliverable: a recommendation with an ADR covering the chosen execution path, the interface contract it exposes (for the Dashboard, pipeline steps, and the Llama Stack eval provider), and the implications for each downstream work stream.\n\n## Scope\n- Assess LMEvalJob's current capabilities and limitations: evaluation against KServe-served and Llama Stack-served models, benchmark suite selection, custom task support, result emission, concurrency.\n- Assess what Llama Stack's eval provider API requires of a backing service.\n- Evaluate build-on-LMEvalJob vs. new-service options against: HLR coverage, Tech Preview → supported path, maintenance cost, interface stability for consumers.\n- Define the interface contract of the chosen path (launch API / CR surface and result retrieval) that downstream consumers build against.\n- Bounded outcomes: (A) extend LMEvalJob as the single execution path; (B) build a new evaluation service. Recommendation selects the corresponding implementation branch.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A recommendation document/ADR exists that chooses between extending LMEvalJob and building a new evaluation service, with evaluation criteria and evidence.\n- The ADR specifies the interface contract (launch surface and result retrieval) downstream consumers (Dashboard, pipeline step, Llama Stack eval provider) build against.\n- For each bounded outcome, the impact on downstream work streams is described.\n- Evidence gathered from the LMEvalJob CRD/operator source and Llama Stack eval provider API (both public) is cited.\n\n## HLR Traceability\nGates HLR-1, HLR-2, HLR-4, HLR-7, HLR-8 (determines the execution path that delivers them); indirectly shapes HLR-5 and HLR-6 via the interface contract.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n- Recommendation requires architecture sign-off; treat the ADR decision as a human gate after the technical analysis.\n\n**E002 body:**\n\n# Investigation: Canonical evaluation result schema for Model Registry persistence\n\n## Description\nResolve the strategy's second open question: the canonical format for evaluation results persisted to Model Registry model-version custom properties, validated against real MMLU output before committing, including behavior under benchmark-suite version bumps.\n\n## Scope\n- Prototype persisting real lm-evaluation-harness MMLU output into Model Registry model version custom properties.\n- Define the canonical result schema: per-benchmark scores, run metadata, suite/task identity and versions.\n- Verify the schema survives benchmark-suite version bumps (versioning fields, compatibility).\n- Validate readability for downstream consumers (Dashboard display, pipeline gating).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A canonical result schema is documented and validated by persisting real MMLU evaluation output to a Model Registry model version.\n- Schema round-trips: persisted results can be read back and interpreted (scores, per-benchmark breakdown, suite version).\n- Version-bump behavior is specified: schema explicitly records benchmark suite/task versions so results remain interpretable across suite upgrades.\n- Compatibility with Model Registry custom-property constraints is demonstrated.\n\n## HLR Traceability\nGates HLR-3 (persistence format); determines the score display format for HLR-5 and the gating data for HLR-6.\n\n**E003 body:**\n\n# Investigation: Benchmark dataset licensing for disconnected redistribution\n\n## Description\nResolve the strategy's third open question: which standard benchmark suites (e.g. MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag exposed through lm-eval task names) can be redistributed by the product in disconnected environments, and which must be fetched by the customer.\n\n## Scope\n- Enumerate the standard suite datasets behind the out-of-the-box lm-eval task names.\n- For each, review the dataset license for redistribution rights in disconnected/disconnected-install contexts.\n- Classify each suite: redistributable (bundle) vs. customer-fetched (documented fetch path).\n- Identify any suites with unclear licensing that need legal review.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A classification list covering every out-of-the-box benchmark suite: redistributable vs. customer-fetched, with the governing license cited.\n- Suites requiring legal confirmation are explicitly flagged with the question to be answered.\n- The classification is sufficient to fix the out-of-the-box suite set for the evaluation execution path.\n\n## HLR Traceability\nGates HLR-2 (determines the out-of-the-box suite set and whether a customer fetch mechanism is needed).\n\n## Risks / Notes\n- Final redistribution approval is a legal/compliance judgment; the technical classification from license texts should be routed for legal sign-off.\n\n**E006 body:**\n\n# Model Registry: evaluation result persistence and model version linking\n\n## Description\nEnable Model Registry to persist evaluation results and link them to the registered version of the evaluated model, using the canonical result schema.\n\n## Scope\n- Implement persistence of evaluation results on ModelVersion custom properties per the canonical result schema.\n- Define the linking semantics between an evaluation result and the evaluated model version (and the serving endpoint/distribution evaluated, where recorded).\n- Support retrieval/query of evaluation results for a model version for downstream consumers (Dashboard comparison, pipeline gating).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Evaluation results in the canonical schema can be persisted on a model version and read back via the Model Registry REST API.\n- Each persisted result is linked to the evaluated model version such that a version's evaluation history is retrievable.\n- Side-by-side retrieval for two model versions is supported (input to the Dashboard comparison view).\n- Persisted results remain interpretable across benchmark-suite version bumps per the schema's versioning fields.\n\n## HLR Traceability\nHLR-3 (P0). Consumes the canonical schema; enables HLR-5 (comparison data) and HLR-6 (gating data).\n\n**E007 body:**\n\n# Dashboard: evaluation runs, scores, and model version comparison\n\n## Description\nDashboard integration showing evaluation runs, their status, per-benchmark scores, and side-by-side comparison of two model versions.\n\n## Scope\n- Evaluation section in the Dashboard: list evaluation runs with status.\n- Per-run view with per-benchmark scores.\n- Side-by-side comparison of two model versions' evaluation results.\n- Reads evaluation run state from the platform evaluation surface selected by the execution engine decision, and scores from Model Registry persisted results.\n- Feature flag / OdhDashboardConfig integration per Dashboard conventions.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Users can view evaluation runs for a project with live status.\n- Users can view per-benchmark scores for a run.\n- Users can select two model versions and compare their evaluation results side by side.\n- Views render correctly against both a running and a completed evaluation run.\n- Integration follows Dashboard conventions (navigation, feature flag via OdhDashboardConfig).\n\n## HLR Traceability\nHLR-5 (P1).\n\n**E008 body:**\n\n# Data Science Pipelines: evaluation step for promotion-gating flows\n\n## Description\nAllow evaluations to be embedded as a step in a Data Science Pipeline so promotion flows can gate on scores.\n\n## Scope\n- Provide a pipeline step/component that launches an evaluation of a served model against a selected benchmark suite.\n- Support gating downstream pipeline steps on evaluation scores (e.g. threshold checks against persisted results).\n- Follow KFP v2 component patterns.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A pipeline can include an evaluation step that runs against a served model and completes with the evaluation's status.\n- Downstream steps can gate on the evaluation's scores (pass/fail on a threshold).\n- The step works with both standard and custom evaluation tasks.\n- Documented example of a promotion-gating flow (gate, not auto-promote — automatic promotion is out of scope).\n\n## HLR Traceability\nHLR-6 (P1).\n\n**E009 body:**\n\n# Documentation: end-to-end model evaluation workflow\n\n## Description\nProduct documentation covering the evaluation workflow end to end, including custom task authoring.\n\n## Scope (content outline inside Scope)\n- Evaluation concepts: benchmark suites, evaluation runs, results and model version linkage.\n- Running an evaluation against a served model (KServe endpoint or Llama Stack distribution).\n- Out-of-the-box suites vs. customer-fetched datasets (per licensing classification).\n- Authoring custom evaluation tasks (own datasets and metrics).\n- Comparing model versions in the Dashboard.\n- Embedding evaluation in pipelines for promotion gating.\n- Cost/duration metadata interpretation for capacity planning.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Documentation covers the full evaluation workflow end to end, including custom task authoring.\n- Technically reviewed against the implemented behavior (accuracy validation against architecture context and the shipped capability).\n- Published in the RHOAI product documentation set.\n\n## HLR Traceability\nHLR-9 (P2). Priority P0 derived from strategy priority Critical per docs-authoring exception.\n\n**BRANCH-A-E004 body:**\n\n# TrustyAI: extend LMEvalJob into the platform evaluation execution path\n\n## Description\nOutcome A of the execution engine investigation: build on TrustyAI's LMEvalJob