{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-ba6db401-7cec-42bf-9a47-bc6171e42d3a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b7bae8feb8ee7fcb","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9002-ncquu2zu/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9002-E002.md","content":"# Investigation: Canonical evaluation result schema for Model Registry persistence\n\n## Description\n\nResolve the strategy's second open question: what is the canonical result format\npersisted to Model Registry model-version custom properties, and does it survive\nbenchmark-suite version bumps? The deliverable is a validated schema — prototyped\nagainst real MMLU output from lm-evaluation-harness before committing — that all\nproducers and consumers of evaluation results build against.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Run a real lm-evaluation-harness MMLU evaluation and capture its raw output\n  (lm-eval is pip-installable and runs locally).\n- Prototype persisting that output into Model Registry model-version custom\n  properties, working within the custom-property extension point documented in\n  the platform architecture context.\n- Define the canonical result schema: per-benchmark scores, aggregate scores, run\n  metadata, and benchmark suite/task identity with versions.\n- Verify the schema survives benchmark-suite version bumps: version fields keep\n  results interpretable when suites upgrade.\n- Confirm the schema is readable by the downstream consumers: Dashboard display\n  (per-benchmark scores, side-by-side comparison) and pipeline score gating.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A canonical evaluation result schema is documented and validated by persisting\n  real MMLU evaluation output to a Model Registry model version's custom\n  properties.\n- The schema round-trips: a persisted result can be read back via the Model\n  Registry REST API and fully interpreted (per-benchmark scores, run metadata,\n  suite/task 