{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-963988f1-e5b4-4287-845d-df30b3ff23c4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8c2b5d1832a4b036","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9002-ncquu2zu/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E006.md","content":"---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E006\ntitle: 'TrustyAI: custom evaluation tasks on the new evaluation service'\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9002\ncomponent: TrustyAI\nteam: TrustyAI\ntype: Implementation\npriority: P1\nbranch: B\ngated_by: RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\ndependencies:\n- RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n- RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E004\ngate_failure_impact:\n  action: rewrite\n  fallback_approach: If the execution engine investigation recommends extending\n    LMEvalJob instead of building a new evaluation service, this epic is replaced\n    by the branch-A custom-task epic built on the LMEvalJob execution path.\nai_signals:\n  change_specificity: 1\n  pattern_precedent: 1\n  adapter_pattern: 1\n  existing_foundation: 0\n  open_questions: -1\n  external_dependency: 1\n  human_process_gates: 0\n  repo_access: 1\n  architecture_claims: 1\nimplementation_type: null\ninvestigation_signals: null\nai_implementability: null\nai_implementability_score: null\njira_key: null\n---\n# TrustyAI: Custom evaluation tasks on the new evaluation service\n\n## Description\n\nBranch outcome B of the execution engine investigation, follow-on to the\nevaluation service epic: users can define custom evaluation tasks — their own\ndatasets and metrics — and run them through the same platform evaluation flow\nserved by the new evaluation service. Custom task support is a distinct,\ndeferrable feature (P1): it is split from the P0 service delivery so the\nstandard-suite capability can ship without it, and this epic extends the\nservice's existing launch surface via a dependency rather than rebuilding it.\nThis epic applies if the investigation recommends a new evaluation service; if\nit recommends extending LMEvalJob instead, this epic is replaced by the\nalternative branch's custom-task epic.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Support user-defined evaluation tasks — custom datasets and custom metrics —\n  accepted by the evaluation service and executed through the same flow as the\n  out-of-the-box standard suites.\n- Accept user-supplied task definitions (dataset and metric) without changes\n  to the service's deployment or serving configuration.\n- Emit custom-task results in the canonical result schema, written through the\n  Model Registry persistence and linking contract so they are linked to the\n  evaluated model's registered version alongside standard-suite results.\n- Record cost/duration metadata (tokens, wall-clock, GPU time) on custom-task\n  runs, using the same run record as standard-suite runs.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A user can define a custom evaluation task (own dataset, own metric) and\n  launch it against a model served on the platform through the same flow used\n  for standard suites.\n- A custom-task run's status is observable while it executes, like a\n  standard-suite run.\n- Custom-task results are persisted in the canonical result schema, linked to\n  the evaluated model's Model Registry version, with cost/duration metadata\n  recorded.\n- Defining a custom task requires no change to the service's deployment or\n  serving configuration.\n- Changed repositories' build pipelines are green.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\nHLR-4 (P1): users can define custom evaluation tasks (own datasets and\nmetrics) and run them through the same flow. Split from the P0 service epic\nso it can be deferred independently of the standard-suite launch/execution\ncore; it extends the service surface that epic delivers.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- The foundation is the newly delivered evaluation service, which is less\n  proven in production than the LMEvalJob path; custom-task support must track\n  the service's API as it stabilizes.\n- User-supplied datasets and metrics may need validation/sandboxing\n  considerations in the service; carried inside this epic.\n- The single-gate schema records only the execution-engine existence gate;\n  this epic also builds against the canonical result schema and the Model\n  Registry persistence contract produced by the upstream investigations,\n  carried as its dependency chain.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":29342,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-82c057e624c263f6","agent_id":"a3d15f7f115c69d80","session_id":"b202a2e5-2f2e-4be7-874a-783267f0136d","uuid":"a5a0e506-4c27-43b1-894b-ab84fc06ced2","timestamp":"2026-10-22T13:52:45.035Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9002 decomposition"}
