{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bba5575ec521f123","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9002\n3\tepic_count: 7\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# RHAISTRAT-9002 Decomposition — Integrated GenAI model evaluation for RHOAI\n11\t\n12\tThe strategy introduces a first-class GenAI model evaluation capability across five\n13\tcomponents (TrustyAI, Llama Stack, Dashboard, Data Science Pipelines, Model Registry).\n14\tAll five are in the platform inventory (architecture context files exist), so no\n15\tonboarding chains are generated. The strategy carries three explicit open questions;\n16\tall three resolve unknowns that change which downstream epics exist or what they do,\n17\tso each becomes an Investigation epic. The execution-engine question has two bounded\n18\toutcomes, decomposed as conditional branches.\n19\t\n20\tNo Staff Engineer Input section is present in the strategy; no overrides to log.\n21\t\n22\t## Epic List\n23\t\n24\tMain-plan epics:\n25\t\n26\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n27\t|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-E001 | Investigation: evaluation execution engine — extend LMEvalJob vs. new evaluation service | Investigation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P0 |\n29\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-E002 | Investigation: canonical evaluation result schema for Model Registry persistence | Investigation | Model Registry | Model Registry | P0 |\n30\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-E003 | Investigation: benchmark dataset licensing for disconnected redistribution | Investigation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P0 |\n31\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-E006 | Model Registry: evaluation result persistence and model version linking | Implementation | Model Registry | Model Registry | P0 |\n32\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-E007 | Dashboard: evaluation runs, scores, and model version comparison | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n33\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-E008 | Data Science Pipelines: evaluation step for promotion-gating flows | Implementation | RHOAI Pipelines | Data Science Pipelines | P1 |\n34\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-E009 | Documentation: end-to-end model evaluation workflow | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | Documentation | P0 |\n35\t\n36\tBranch-conditional epics (alternatives selected by the E001 outcome; attached to the\n37\tE001 investigation as a plan document, not standalone Jira issues):\n38\t\n39\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n40\t|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n41\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-A-E004 | TrustyAI: extend LMEvalJob into the platform evaluation execution path | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P0 |\n42\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-A-E005 | Llama Stack: eval provider backed by the TrustyAI execution path | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P1 |\n43\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-A-E006 | TrustyAI: custom evaluation tasks on the LMEvalJob execution path | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P1 |\n44\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E004 | TrustyAI: new platform evaluation service | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P0 |\n45\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E005 | Llama Stack: eval provider backed by the new evaluation service | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P1 |\n46\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E006 | TrustyAI: custom evaluation tasks on the new evaluation service | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P1 |\n47\t\n48\t## Dependency DAG\n49\t\n50\tArrows point from dependency to dependent (`X --> Y` means X must complete before Y starts).\n51\t\n52\t```mermaid\n53\tgraph TD\n54\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: execution engine decision\"]\n55\t    E002[\"E002 Investigation: result schema\"]\n56\t    E003[\"E003 Investigation: suite licensing\"]\n57\t    E006[\"E006 Model Registry persistence & linking\"]\n58\t    E007[\"E007 Dashboard eval views\"]\n59\t    E008[\"E008 Pipelines eval step\"]\n60\t    E009[\"E009 Documentation\"]\n61\t    BA4[\"BRANCH-A-E004 extend LMEvalJob\"]\n62\t    BA5[\"BRANCH-A-E005 Llama Stack provider (A)\"]\n63\t    BA6[\"BRANCH-A-E006 custom tasks (A)\"]\n64\t    BB4[\"BRANCH-B-E004 new eval service\"]\n65\t    BB5[\"BRANCH-B-E005 Llama Stack provider (B)\"]\n66\t    BB6[\"BRANCH-B-E006 custom tasks (B)\"]\n67\t\n68\t    E002 --> E006\n69\t    E001 --> E007\n70\t    E006 --> E007\n71\t    E001 --> E008\n72\t    E006 --> E008\n73\t    E006 --> E009\n74\t    E007 --> E009\n75\t    E008 --> E009\n76\t\n77\t    E001 --> BA4\n78\t    E002 --> BA4\n79\t    E003 --> BA4\n80\t    E006 --> BA4\n81\t    E001 --> BA5\n82\t    BA4 --> BA5\n83\t    E001 --> BA6\n84\t    BA4 --> BA6\n85\t\n86\t    E001 --> BB4\n87\t    E002 --> BB4\n88\t    E003 --> BB4\n89\t    E006 --> BB4\n90\t    E001 --> BB5\n91\t    BB4 --> BB5\n92\t    E001 --> BB6\n93\t    BB4 --> BB6\n94\t```\n95\t\n96\t## DAG Justification\n97\t\n98\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n99\t|------|------|-----------|\n100\t| E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003 (no edges) | Rule 14 | Three independent investigations with independent inputs; each starts immediately. |\n101\t| E002 → E006 | Rule 3 / Rule 13 | The result-schema investigation produces the canonical schema that the Model Registry persistence work builds against; the schema decision rewrites the persistence epic's approach. |\n102\t| E001 → E007, E001 → E008 | Rule 3 | Both consumers build against the evaluation surface (launch API / run CR) selected by the execution-engine decision; gated_by with `rewrite` — the epic exists either way but its data-source integration changes with the outcome. Per the branch rules, main-plan epics depend on the Investigation outcome, never on a branch epic. |\n103\t| E006 → E007, E006 → E008 | Rule 13 | Dashboard comparison and pipeline score gating consume the persisted, linked results the Model Registry epic produces. |\n104\t| E006/E007/E008 → E009 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring is blocked by every main-plan Implementation epic; docs describe what was built. Branch epics are excluded from this requirement (they are conditional plan documents, not main-plan nodes). |\n105\t| E001 → BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 | Rule 3 | True gate: the investigation outcome selects which branch epic exists (`rewrite`); each branch epic's `gated_by` points at E001. |\n106\t| E002 → BRANCH-*-E004 | Rule 13 | The execution engine emits results in the canonical schema — a build-against artifact. (Also transitively covered via E006; kept explicit for traceability.) |\n107\t| E003 → BRANCH-*-E004 | Rule 3 | The licensing classification fixes the out-of-the-box suite set (bundled vs. customer-fetched), i.e. the HLR-2 scope of the execution epic. |\n108\t| E006 → BRANCH-*-E004 | Rule 13 | The engine persists results through the Model Registry persistence/linking contract. |\n109\t| BRANCH-*-E004 → BRANCH-*-E005 | Rule 12 / Rule 13 | The Llama Stack eval provider is a consumer of the execution engine's service API; it builds against that backend, so it is serial with the engine and lives inside the same branch (it depends on specific branch work, not just the investigation outcome). |\n110\t| E001 → BRANCH-*-E005 | Rule 3 (gated_by membership) | `gated_by` must appear in `dependencies`; the provider wiring is itself rewritten by the engine decision. |\n111\t| BRANCH-*-E004 → BRANCH-*-E006 | Rule 13 | Custom-task support (HLR-4, P1) extends the launch flow and run record delivered by the branch execution epic; it builds against that surface, so it is serial within the branch and lives in the same branch. Split out so the P1 feature can be deferred independently of the P0 execution core. |\n112\t| E001 → BRANCH-*-E006 | Rule 3 (gated_by membership) | `gated_by` must appear in `dependencies`; under the other engine outcome the custom-task epic is replaced by its branch alternative. |\n113\t| E007 ∥ E008 | Rule 14 | Different components, different repos, no shared artifacts. |\n114\t| Branch A ∥ Branch B | — | Mutually exclusive alternatives, never both executed. |\n115\t\n116\tNotable non-edges:\n117\t\n118\t- No `konflux-onboarding`, `repo-onboarding`, or `license-validation` epics: all five\n119\t  affected components exist in the platform inventory and are Konflux-onboarded\n120\t  per architecture context. (Branch B notes repo provisioning contingently, inside\n121\t  the epic, if the new service needs its own repository.)\n122\t- No edge between the Dashboard/Pipelines epics and the branch engine epics:\n123\t  main-plan epics must not depend on branch epics; both consume the E001 outcome\n124\t  (the selected evaluation surface) plus the stable E006 persistence contract.\n125\t- No tiered delivery: no external dependency with uncertain timing gates user value\n126\t  (the possible Llama Stack upstream coordination is carried as a risk on the\n127\t  provider epics, not a delivery tier).\n128\t\n129\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n130\t\n131\t| HLR | Priority | Covering epic(s) | Notes |\n132\t|-----|----------|------------------|-------|\n133\t| HLR-1 | P0 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 | Execution path selected by E001. |\n134\t| HLR-2 | P0 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 | Out-of-the-box suite set fixed by E003 (licensing). |\n135\t| HLR-3 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9002-E006 | Schema from E002; result writing performed by the execution epic. |\n136\t| HLR-4 | P1 | BRANCH-A-E006 / BRANCH-B-E006 | Custom-task support split from the P0 execution epic into a per-branch P1 epic so it can be deferred independently; extends the branch execution surface via a dependency edge. |\n137\t| HLR-5 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9002-E007 | |\n138\t| HLR-6 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9002-E008 | |\n139\t| HLR-7 | P1 | BRANCH-A-E005 / BRANCH-B-E005 | Provider backend determined by the E001 outcome. |\n140\t| HLR-8 | P2 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 | Bundled with the P0 execution epic — cost/duration metadata falls out of the same run record. |\n141\t| HLR-9 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9002-E009 | docs-authoring; priority P0 via strategy priority Critical → P0 mapping. |\n142\t\n143\tAll nine HLRs are covered; none orphaned.\n144\t\n145\t### Priority bundling notes\n146\t\n147\tHLR-4 (P1, custom tasks) is **not** bundled into the P0 execution epics: custom task\n148\tauthoring is a distinct, deferrable user-facing feature, and independent deferral is a\n149\tplanning requirement that overrides boundary convenience. It is covered by per-branch\n150\tP1 epics (BRANCH-*-E006) that depend on the branch execution epic — the split extends\n151\tthe P0 epic's surface via a dependency edge rather than rebuilding the execution path\n152\tindependently, so no boundary duplication is introduced.\n153\t\n154\tHLR-8 (P2, cost metadata) remains bundled into the P0 execution epic: cost/duration\n155\tmetadata genuinely falls out of the same run record as the P0 launch/execution work\n156\tand has no separate user-facing surface, so splitting it would add an epic without a\n157\tdeferral decision to make.\n158\t\n159\tThe docs epic (HLR-9, P2 requirement) is P0 by the docs-authoring priority exception:\n160\tthe strategy frontmatter declares `priority: Critical` → P0. It is exempt from\n161\tpushing priority upstream (its P1 dependents are unaffected).\n162\t\n163\t## Conditional Branches\n164\t\n165\tThe execution-engine investigation (E001) has two bounded outcomes:\n166\t\n167\t- **Branch A — extend LMEvalJob**: TrustyAI's LMEvalJob (Tech Preview) becomes the\n168\t  single execution path. Epics `BRANCH-A-E004` (engine), `BRANCH-A-E005`\n169\t  (Llama Stack eval provider backed by that path), and `BRANCH-A-E006`\n170\t  (custom evaluation tasks on that path, P1).\n171\t- **Branch B — new evaluation service**: a new evaluation service wraps\n172\t  lm-evaluation-harness and serves both the platform flow and the Llama Stack eval\n173\t  provider API. Epics `BRANCH-B-E004` (service), `BRANCH-B-E005` (provider\n174\t  backed by the service), and `BRANCH-B-E006` (custom evaluation tasks on the\n175\t  service, P1).\n176\t\n177\tBranch epics set `branch`, `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-9002-E001`, `gate_failure_impact`\n178\t(rewrite — if the other outcome is chosen, the epic is replaced by its alternative),\n179\tand include E001 in `dependencies`. No main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its\n180\tdependencies. Branch epics are excluded from the main-plan critical path.\n181\t\n182\t## Component Mapping Notes\n183\t\n184\t- All epic `component` values are copied verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`.\n185\t- Branch B's new evaluation service has no canonical component of its own; it is\n186\t  mapped to **TrustyAI** (the platform's model-quality/trustworthiness service\n187\t  family, which already owns lm-eval execution via LMEvalJob). If the service ships\n188\t  as a new repository, repo provisioning is handled inside the branch epic.\n189\t- KServe and Serving Runtimes are consumption targets of the evaluation engine\n190\t  (HLR-1 mentions KServe endpoints) but are not in the strategy's Affected\n191\t  Components list; no epics are generated for them.\n192\t\n193\t## Health Warnings\n194\t\n195\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n196\t  Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no Non-Functional\n197\t  Requirements. Teams were inferred from architecture context owning teams; epic\n198\t  acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs. The Documentation epic's owning\n199\t  team (\"RHOAI Documentation\") is an assumption — no source states it.\n200\t- **Priority inversion risk (flagged, not fixed)**: the docs epic lands at P0 via\n201\t  the Critical→P0 mapping while covering only a P2 requirement (HLR-9). This is the\n202\t  specified rule, but planners should not treat the documentation as P0-urgent work.\n203\t- **Human gates inside investigations**: the E001 recommendation requires\n204\t  architecture sign-off (ADR), and the E003 licensing classification needs\n205\t  legal/compliance confirmation before suites are bundled. Both are noted on the\n206\t  epics; neither changes the DAG structure.\n207\t- **Branch B greenfield risk**: a new evaluation service is higher-risk than\n208\t  extending LMEvalJob; E001's recommendation must weigh maintainability and the\n209\t  Tech Preview to supported path explicitly. (See the branch epic's risk notes.)\n210\t- **Possible upstream coordination** on the Llama Stack provider epics\n211\t  (meta-llama/llama-stack) if the provider cannot be carried entirely midstream;\n212\t  carried as a risk, not a separate epic (Rule 21: no accepted upstream PR/RFC is\n213\t  required by the strategy).\n214\t\n215\t## Tiered Delivery\n216\t\n217\tNot applicable — no external dependency with uncertain timing gates delivery of\n218\tuser-facing value. All Investigations start immediately in parallel; partial value\n219\t(their resolutions) arrives regardless of branch outcome.\n220\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8e3df1c7c773e287","agent_id":"a7a33c193715a6f3c","session_id":"b202a2e5-2f2e-4be7-874a-783267f0136d","uuid":"4a4856c3-9349-49ba-b884-afc60cb3aca2","timestamp":"2026-10-22T13:56:13.203Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-bba5575ec521f123","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9002 decomposition"}
