{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b6e1bb85a5364d7d","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E006\n3\ttitle: 'TrustyAI: custom evaluation tasks on the new evaluation service'\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9002\n5\tcomponent: TrustyAI\n6\tteam: TrustyAI\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P1\n9\tbranch: B\n10\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n11\tdependencies:\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E004\n14\tgate_failure_impact:\n15\t  action: rewrite\n16\t  fallback_approach: If the execution engine investigation recommends extending LMEvalJob\n17\t    instead of building a new evaluation service, this epic is replaced by the branch-A\n18\t    custom-task epic built on the LMEvalJob execution path.\n19\tai_signals:\n20\t  change_specificity: 1\n21\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n22\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n23\t  existing_foundation: 0\n24\t  open_questions: -1\n25\t  external_dependency: 1\n26\t  human_process_gates: 0\n27\t  repo_access: 1\n28\t  architecture_claims: 1\n29\timplementation_type: null\n30\tinvestigation_signals: null\n31\tai_implementability: High\n32\tai_implementability_score: 5\n33\tjira_key: null\n34\t---\n35\t# TrustyAI: Custom evaluation tasks on the new evaluation service\n36\t\n37\t## Description\n38\t\n39\tBranch outcome B of the execution engine investigation, follow-on to the\n40\tevaluation service epic: users can define custom evaluation tasks — their own\n41\tdatasets and metrics — and run them through the same platform evaluation flow\n42\tserved by the new evaluation service. Custom task support is a distinct,\n43\tdeferrable feature (P1): it is split from the P0 service delivery so the\n44\tstandard-suite capability can ship without it, and this epic extends the\n45\tservice's existing launch surface via a dependency rather than rebuilding it.\n46\tThis epic applies if the investigation recommends a new evaluation service; if\n47\tit recommends extending LMEvalJob instead, this epic is replaced by the\n48\talternative branch's custom-task epic.\n49\t\n50\t## Scope\n51\t\n52\t- Support user-defined evaluation tasks — custom datasets and custom metrics —\n53\t  accepted by the evaluation service and executed through the same flow as the\n54\t  out-of-the-box standard suites.\n55\t- Accept user-supplied task definitions (dataset and metric) without changes\n56\t  to the service's deployment or serving configuration.\n57\t- Emit custom-task results in the canonical result schema, written through the\n58\t  Model Registry persistence and linking contract so they are linked to the\n59\t  evaluated model's registered version alongside standard-suite results.\n60\t- Record cost/duration metadata (tokens, wall-clock, GPU time) on custom-task\n61\t  runs, using the same run record as standard-suite runs.\n62\t\n63\t## Acceptance Criteria\n64\t\n65\t- A user can define a custom evaluation task (own dataset, own metric) and\n66\t  launch it against a model served on the platform through the same flow used\n67\t  for standard suites.\n68\t- A custom-task run's status is observable while it executes, like a\n69\t  standard-suite run.\n70\t- Custom-task results are persisted in the canonical result schema, linked to\n71\t  the evaluated model's Model Registry version, with cost/duration metadata\n72\t  recorded.\n73\t- Defining a custom task requires no change to the service's deployment or\n74\t  serving configuration.\n75\t- Changed repositories' build pipelines are green.\n76\t\n77\t## HLR Traceability\n78\t\n79\tHLR-4 (P1): users can define custom evaluation tasks (own datasets and\n80\tmetrics) and run them through the same flow. Split from the P0 service epic\n81\tso it can be deferred independently of the standard-suite launch/execution\n82\tcore; it extends the service surface that epic delivers.\n83\t\n84\t## Risks / Notes\n85\t\n86\t- The foundation is the newly delivered evaluation service, which is less\n87\t  proven in production than the LMEvalJob path; custom-task support must track\n88\t  the service's API as it stabilizes.\n89\t- User-supplied datasets and metrics may need validation/sandboxing\n90\t  considerations in the service; carried inside this epic.\n91\t- The single-gate schema records only the execution-engine existence gate;\n92\t  this epic also builds against the canonical result schema and the Model\n93\t  Registry persistence contract produced by the upstream investigations,\n94\t  carried as its dependency chain.\n95\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8e3df1c7c773e287","agent_id":"a7a33c193715a6f3c","session_id":"b202a2e5-2f2e-4be7-874a-783267f0136d","uuid":"e09c4dac-1988-494f-ad61-d12453f96586","timestamp":"2026-10-22T13:56:44.077Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b6e1bb85a5364d7d","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9002 decomposition"}
