{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b2f21c7318df236d","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E004\n3\ttitle: 'TrustyAI: new platform evaluation service'\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9002\n5\tcomponent: TrustyAI\n6\tteam: TrustyAI\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tbranch: B\n10\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n11\tdependencies:\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E002\n14\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E003\n15\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E006\n16\tgate_failure_impact:\n17\t  action: rewrite\n18\t  fallback_approach: If the execution engine investigation recommends extending LMEvalJob\n19\t    instead of building a new service, this epic is replaced by the branch-A LMEvalJob\n20\t    extension.\n21\tai_signals:\n22\t  change_specificity: -1\n23\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n24\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n25\t  existing_foundation: -1\n26\t  open_questions: -1\n27\t  external_dependency: 1\n28\t  human_process_gates: 0\n29\t  repo_access: 1\n30\t  architecture_claims: 1\n31\timplementation_type: null\n32\tinvestigation_signals: null\n33\tai_implementability: Medium\n34\tai_implementability_score: 1\n35\tjira_key: null\n36\t---\n37\t# TrustyAI: New platform evaluation service\n38\t\n39\t## Description\n40\t\n41\tBranch outcome B of the execution engine investigation: build a new evaluation\n42\tservice as the platform's execution path for model evaluation. The service\n43\twraps lm-evaluation-harness, executes benchmark runs against models served on\n44\tthe platform (KServe endpoints or Llama Stack distributions), persists results\n45\tin the canonical schema via the Model Registry persistence contract linked to\n46\tModel Registry versions, and exposes a service API suitable as the backend for\n47\tLlama Stack's eval provider API. Custom evaluation tasks (user-defined\n48\tdatasets and metrics) are delivered by a follow-on P1 epic in this branch,\n49\tdeferred independently of this P0 core. This epic applies if the investigation recommends a new service; if it recommends\n50\textending LMEvalJob instead, this epic is replaced by the alternative branch's\n51\tLMEvalJob extension.\n52\t\n53\t## Scope\n54\t\n55\t- Build the evaluation service: execution of lm-eval benchmark runs against\n56\t  models served on KServe endpoints or Llama Stack distributions.\n57\t- Define and implement the launch surface (how an evaluation is started) and\n58\t  run-status observation for consumers.\n59\t- Deliver the out-of-the-box standard suite set fixed by the benchmark\n60\t  licensing classification: redistributable suites bundled for disconnected\n61\t  environments; customer-fetched suites via a documented fetch path.\n62\t- Emit results in the canonical result schema and write them through the Model\n63\t  Registry persistence and linking contract (owned by the Model Registry\n64\t  persistence work), which links them to the evaluated model's registered\n65\t  version; this epic does not implement its own result storage.\n66\t- Record cost/duration metadata per run: tokens, wall-clock, GPU time.\n67\t- Expose a service API suitable as the Llama Stack eval provider backend.\n68\t- If the service requires its own repository, include midstream repo\n69\t  provisioning and build-pipeline onboarding; otherwise deliver it within the\n70\t  existing TrustyAI midstream repositories.\n71\t\n72\t## Acceptance Criteria\n73\t\n74\t- A user can launch an evaluation of a model served on the platform (KServe\n75\t  endpoint or Llama Stack distribution) against a selected benchmark suite,\n76\t  and observe the run's status while it executes.\n77\t- Standard suites classified as redistributable are available out of the box in\n78\t  a disconnected environment; customer-fetched suites have a documented fetch\n79\t  path.\n80\t- Results are emitted in the canonical result schema, written through the\n81\t  Model Registry persistence contract, and linked to the evaluated model's\n82\t  Model Registry version.\n83\t- Every run records cost/duration metadata: tokens, wall-clock, GPU time.\n84\t- The service API is sufficient to back Llama Stack's eval provider API.\n85\t- The delivery vehicle is decided and, if a new repository is required, it is\n86\t  provisioned with a green build pipeline.\n87\t\n88\t## HLR Traceability\n89\t\n90\tHLR-1 (P0): launch an evaluation of a served model against a selected suite.\n91\tHLR-2 (P0): standard benchmark suites available out of the box. HLR-8 (P2):\n92\tcost/duration metadata per run — bundled with the P0 work because it falls out\n93\tof the same run record. HLR-4 (P1, custom evaluation tasks) is covered by the\n94\tfollow-on custom-task epic in this branch, split out so it can be deferred\n95\tindependently of the P0 core.\n96\t\n97\t## Risks / Notes\n98\t\n99\t- Greenfield service: higher implementation and maintenance risk than\n100\t  extending the existing LMEvalJob path; the investigation's recommendation\n101\t  must have weighed this explicitly.\n102\t- The service is mapped to the TrustyAI component (the platform's model\n103\t  quality service family); there is no separate canonical component for an\n104\t  evaluation service.\n105\t- The single-gate schema records only the execution-engine existence gate;\n106\t  this epic's suite-delivery scope is also materially conditioned on the\n107\t  benchmark licensing classification (redistributable vs. customer-fetched)\n108\t  and on the canonical result schema from the upstream investigations, both\n109\t  carried as plain build-against dependencies.\n110\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a374a083ce15c2b1","agent_id":"a26eaf71c516d7bc2","session_id":"b202a2e5-2f2e-4be7-874a-783267f0136d","uuid":"598ea135-7749-41d9-bfbc-3bb3b719d3cf","timestamp":"2026-10-22T14:07:36.707Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b2f21c7318df236d","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Final review of RHAISTRAT-9002"}
