{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b18fe89520f3c1ec","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t===== artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9002-E001.md =====\n2\t---\n3\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n4\ttitle: 'Investigation: evaluation execution engine — extend LMEvalJob vs. new evaluation\n5\t  service'\n6\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9002\n7\tcomponent: TrustyAI\n8\tteam: TrustyAI\n9\ttype: Investigation\n10\tpriority: P0\n11\tinvestigation_signals:\n12\t  question_specificity: 1\n13\t  source_accessibility: 1\n14\t  local_runnability: 1\n15\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: -1\n16\t  human_judgment_required: -1\n17\timplementation_type: null\n18\tdependencies: []\n19\tai_signals: null\n20\tai_implementability: Medium\n21\tai_implementability_score: 1\n22\tjira_key: null\n23\tbranch: null\n24\tgated_by: null\n25\tgate_failure_impact: null\n26\t---\n27\t# Investigation: Evaluation execution engine — extend LMEvalJob vs. new evaluation service\n28\t\n29\t## Description\n30\t\n31\tResolve the strategy's first open question: does the platform build its evaluation\n32\texecution path on TrustyAI's existing LMEvalJob (Tech Preview, already wrapping\n33\tEleutherAI lm-evaluation-harness), or is a new evaluation service needed that can\n34\talso serve Llama Stack's eval provider API? The deliverable is a technical\n35\trecommendation, recorded as an ADR, that selects the execution path and specifies\n36\tthe interface contract downstream consumers (Dashboard views, pipeline steps, and\n37\tthe Llama Stack eval provider) build against.\n38\t\n39\t## Scope\n40\t\n41\t- Assess LMEvalJob's current capabilities and limits: evaluation against models\n42\t  served on KServe endpoints and Llama Stack distributions, benchmark suite\n43\t  selection, custom task support, result emission, and concurrency behavior.\n44\t- Assess what Llama Stack's upstream eval provider API requires of a backing\n45\t  service, and whether the LMEvalJob path can serve it.\n46\t- Compare the two bounded outcomes against: High Level Requirement coverage,\n47\t  moving a Tech Preview capability toward supported status, maintenance cost,\n48\t  and interface stability for consumers.\n49\t- Specify the interface contract of the chosen path: how an evaluation is\n50\t  launched, how run status is observed, and how results are retrieved.\n51\t- Document the bounded outcomes: (A) extend LMEvalJob as the single execution\n52\t  path; (B) build a new evaluation service. The recommendation selects the\n53\t  corresponding implementation branch for the execution engine and the Llama\n54\t  Stack eval provider wiring.\n55\t\n56\t## Acceptance Criteria\n57\t\n58\t- A recommendation document (ADR) exists that chooses between extending LMEvalJob\n59\t  and building a new evaluation service, stating the evaluation criteria and the\n60\t  evidence behind each.\n61\t- The ADR specifies the interface contract (launch surface, run status, result\n62\t  retrieval) that downstream consumers build against.\n63\t- The implications of each bounded outcome for every downstream work stream\n64\t  (execution engine, Llama Stack eval provider, Dashboard, pipeline step) are\n65\t  described.\n66\t- Evidence is cited from the LMEvalJob CRD/operator source, the Llama Stack eval\n67\t  provider API, and the platform architecture context — all readable public\n68\t  artifacts.\n69\t\n70\t## HLR Traceability\n71\t\n72\tGates HLR-1, HLR-2, HLR-4, HLR-7, and HLR-8: it determines the execution path that\n73\tdelivers them and the backend the Llama Stack eval provider is wired to. It also\n74\tshapes HLR-5 and HLR-6 indirectly by fixing the interface contract those consumers\n75\tintegrate with.\n76\t\n77\t## Risks / Notes\n78\t\n79\t- The technical analysis is desk-and-local work, but the recommendation requires\n80\t  architecture sign-off (ADR approval) before the selected branch starts — a\n81\t  human gate on the downstream implementation branches.\n82\t- Validating LMEvalJob behavior against a live served model requires a cluster;\n83\t  the recommendation itself does not.\n84\t===== artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9002-E002.md =====\n85\t---\n86\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9002-E002\n87\ttitle: 'Investigation: canonical evaluation result schema for Model Registry persistence'\n88\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9002\n89\tcomponent: Model Registry\n90\tteam: Model Registry\n91\ttype: Investigation\n92\tpriority: P0\n93\tinvestigation_signals:\n94\t  question_specificity: 1\n95\t  source_accessibility: 1\n96\t  local_runnability: 1\n97\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: 0\n98\t  human_judgment_required: 0\n99\timplementation_type: null\n100\tdependencies: []\n101\tai_signals: null\n102\tai_implementability: High\n103\tai_implementability_score: 3\n104\tjira_key: null\n105\tbranch: null\n106\tgated_by: null\n107\tgate_failure_impact: null\n108\t---\n109\t# Investigation: Canonical evaluation result schema for Model Registry persistence\n110\t\n111\t## Description\n112\t\n113\tResolve the strategy's second open question: what is the canonical result format\n114\tpersisted to Model Registry model-version custom properties, and does it survive\n115\tbenchmark-suite version bumps? The deliverable is a validated schema — prototyped\n116\tagainst real MMLU output from lm-evaluation-harness before committing — that all\n117\tproducers and consumers of evaluation results build against.\n118\t\n119\t## Scope\n120\t\n121\t- Run a real lm-evaluation-harness MMLU evaluation and capture its raw output\n122\t  (lm-eval is pip-installable and runs locally).\n123\t- Prototype persisting that output into Model Registry model-version custom\n124\t  properties, working within the custom-property extension point documented in\n125\t  the platform architecture context.\n126\t- Define the canonical result schema: per-benchmark scores, aggregate scores, run\n127\t  metadata, and benchmark suite/task identity with versions.\n128\t- Verify the schema survives benchmark-suite version bumps: version fields keep\n129\t  results interpretable when suites upgrade.\n130\t- Confirm the schema is readable by the downstream consumers: Dashboard display\n131\t  (per-benchmark scores, side-by-side comparison) and pipeline score gating.\n132\t\n133\t## Acceptance Criteria\n134\t\n135\t- A canonical evaluation result schema is documented and validated by persisting\n136\t  real MMLU evaluation output to a Model Registry model version's custom\n137\t  properties.\n138\t- The schema round-trips: a persisted result can be read back via the Model\n139\t  Registry REST API and fully interpreted (per-benchmark scores, run metadata,\n140\t  suite/task identity).\n141\t- Benchmark-suite version bumps are handled explicitly: the schema records suite\n142\t  and task versions so older results remain interpretable after an upgrade.\n143\t- Compatibility with Model Registry custom-property constraints (size, types) is\n144\t  demonstrated with the real output, not asserted.\n145\t\n146\t## HLR Traceability\n147\t\n148\tGates HLR-3: it produces the persistence format that the Model Registry result\n149\tpersistence work implements. It also determines the score data shape for HLR-5\n150\t(Dashboard display and comparison) and HLR-6 (pipeline score gating).\n151\t\n152\t## Risks / Notes\n153\t\n154\t- No product or stakeholder decision is pending; the schema is an engineering\n155\t  commitment. Cross-team review (TrustyAI as producer, Dashboard and Pipelines\n156\t  as consumers) is part of normal engineering review, not a governance gate.\n157\t===== artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9002-E003.md =====\n158\t---\n159\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9002-E003\n160\ttitle: 'Investigation: benchmark dataset licensing for disconnected redistribution'\n161\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9002\n162\tcomponent: TrustyAI\n163\tteam: TrustyAI\n164\ttype: Investigation\n165\tpriority: P0\n166\tinvestigation_signals:\n167\t  question_specificity: 1\n168\t  source_accessibility: 1\n169\t  local_runnability: 0\n170\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: 0\n171\t  human_judgment_required: -1\n172\timplementation_type: null\n173\tdependencies: []\n174\tai_signals: null\n175\tai_implementability: Medium\n176\tai_implementability_score: 1\n177\tjira_key: null\n178\tbranch: null\n179\tgated_by: null\n180\tgate_failure_impact: null\n181\t---\n182\t# Investigation: Benchmark dataset licensing for disconnected redistribution\n183\t\n184\t## Description\n185\t\n186\tResolve the strategy's third open question: which standard benchmark suites\n187\t(e.g. MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag exposed through lm-eval task names) can be\n188\tredistributed by the product in disconnected environments, and which must be\n189\tfetched by the customer. The deliverable is a classification, per suite, that\n190\tfixes the out-of-the-box suite set and determines whether a customer-fetch\n191\tmechanism is needed.\n192\t\n193\t## Scope\n194\t\n195\t- Enumerate the standard benchmark suite datasets behind the out-of-the-box\n196\t  lm-eval task names the evaluation capability will expose.\n197\t- For each dataset, review its license for redistribution rights, specifically\n198\t  in disconnected-environment (offline install) contexts.\n199\t- Classify each suite: redistributable (can be bundled with the product) vs.\n200\t  customer-fetched (must be downloaded by the customer at install/run time).\n201\t- Flag any suites with ambiguous licensing that need legal or compliance\n202\t  confirmation, with the specific question each raises.\n203\t\n204\t## Acceptance Criteria\n205\t\n206\t- A classification list exists covering every out-of-the-box benchmark suite,\n207\t  labeling each as redistributable or customer-fetched, with the governing\n208\t  license cited for each.\n209\t- Suites requiring legal confirmation are explicitly flagged with the precise\n210\t  question to be answered, so the legal request is actionable.\n211\t- The classification is sufficient to fix the out-of-the-box suite set and to\n212\t  decide whether a customer-fetch mechanism is in scope for the evaluation\n213\t  execution work.\n214\t\n215\t## HLR Traceability\n216\t\n217\tGates HLR-2: it determines which standard suites ship out of the box and which\n218\trequire a customer fetch path — the scope of the out-of-the-box suite delivery\n219\tin the evaluation execution work.\n220\t\n221\t## Risks / Notes\n222\t\n223\t- The classification itself is desk work from public license texts, but final\n224\t  redistribution approval for bundled datasets is a legal/compliance judgment;\n225\t  route the classification for legal sign-off before suites are bundled.\n226\t===== artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9002-E006.md =====\n227\t---\n228\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9002-E006\n229\ttitle: 'Model Registry: evaluation result persistence and model version linking'\n230\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9002\n231\tcomponent: Model Registry\n232\tteam: Model Registry\n233\ttype: Implementation\n234\tpriority: P0\n235\tdependencies:\n236\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E002\n237\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-9002-E002\n238\tgate_failure_impact:\n239\t  action: rewrite\n240\t  fallback_approach: If the schema prototype revises the canonical format or uncovers\n241\t    custom-property constraints, the persistence implementation is rewritten against\n242\t    the validated schema; the epic itself is not removed.\n243\tai_signals:\n244\t  change_specificity: 0\n245\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n246\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n247\t  existing_foundation: 1\n248\t  open_questions: -1\n249\t  external_dependency: 1\n250\t  human_process_gates: 1\n251\t  repo_access: 1\n252\t  architecture_claims: 1\n253\timplementation_type: null\n254\tinvestigation_signals: null\n255\tai_implementability: High\n256\tai_implementability_score: 5\n257\tjira_key: null\n258\tbranch: null\n259\t---\n260\t# Model Registry: Evaluation result persistence and model version linking\n261\t\n262\t## Description\n263\t\n264\tEnable Model Registry to persist evaluation results and link them to the\n265\tregistered version of the evaluated model, implementing the canonical result\n266\tschema so that every evaluation of a model version becomes part of that\n267\tversion's record. This is the persistence half of the strategy's\n268\tresults-alongside-the-model requirement; the evaluation execution path writes\n269\tthrough it.\n270\t\n271\t## Scope\n272\t\n273\t- Own the persistence and query mechanism for evaluation results as a platform\n274\t  contract: the evaluation execution path (under either engine decision)\n275\t  writes results through this contract and does not implement its own result\n276\t  storage; this epic does not own result emission or execution.\n277\t- Implement persistence of evaluation results on ModelVersion custom properties\n278\t  per the canonical result schema (per-benchmark scores, run metadata,\n279\t  suite/task identity and versions).\n280\t- Define the linking semantics between an evaluation result and the evaluated\n281\t  model version, including the identity of what was evaluated (the registered\n282\t  model version) and, where recorded, the serving surface it was evaluated\n283\t  against.\n284\t- Support retrieval of a model version's evaluation history and of results for\n285\t  two model versions side by side, as the query surface for Dashboard\n286\t  comparison and pipeline score gating.\n287\t\n288\t## Acceptance Criteria\n289\t\n290\t- An evaluation result in the canonical schema can be persisted on a registered\n291\t  model version and read back complete via the Model Registry REST API.\n292\t- Each persisted result is linked to the evaluated model version such that a\n293\t  version's full evaluation history is retrievable.\n294\t- Results for two model versions can be retrieved for side-by-side comparison.\n295\t- Persisted results remain interpretable across benchmark-suite version bumps,\n296\t  per the schema's versioning fields.\n297\t- Existing Model Registry behavior and API consumers are not broken by the\n298\t  change.\n299\t\n300\t## HLR Traceability\n301\t\n302\tHLR-3 (P0): evaluation results are persisted and linked to the Model Registry\n303\tversion of the evaluated model. Also enables HLR-5 (comparison data source) and\n304\tHLR-6 (gating data source).\n305\t===== artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9002-E007.md =====\n306\t---\n307\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9002-E007\n308\ttitle: 'Dashboard: evaluation runs, scores, and model version comparison'\n309\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9002\n310\tcomponent: Dashboard\n311\tteam: RHOAI Dashboard\n312\ttype: Implementation\n313\tpriority: P1\n314\tdependencies:\n315\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n316\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E006\n317\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n318\tgate_failure_impact:\n319\t  action: rewrite\n320\t  fallback_approach: The user-facing views are unchanged by the execution engine decision,\n321\t    but the run-status data source (CR surface vs. service API) is rewritten to whichever\n322\t    surface the decision selects.\n323\tai_signals:\n324\t  change_specificity: 0\n325\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n326\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n327\t  existing_foundation: 1\n328\t  open_questions: -1\n329\t  external_dependency: 1\n330\t  human_process_gates: 0\n331\t  repo_access: 1\n332\t  architecture_claims: 1\n333\timplementation_type: null\n334\tinvestigation_signals: null\n335\tai_implementability: High\n336\tai_implementability_score: 5\n337\tjira_key: null\n338\tbranch: null\n339\t---\n340\t# Dashboard: Evaluation runs, scores, and model version comparison\n341\t\n342\t## Description\n343\t\n344\tAdd the evaluation capability to the RHOAI Dashboard: users can see evaluation\n345\truns and their status, view per-benchmark scores, and compare two model versions\n346\tside by side to decide which candidate to promote. The Dashboard reads\n347\tevaluation run state from the platform evaluation surface selected by the\n348\texecution engine decision, and scores from results persisted in Model Registry.\n349\t\n350\t## Scope\n351\t\n352\t- Evaluation views in the Dashboard: list evaluation runs in a data science\n353\t  project with live run status.\n354\t- Per-run detail view showing per-benchmark scores from the persisted results.\n355\t- Side-by-side comparison view for two model versions' evaluation results, so\n356\t  users can weigh candidates before promotion.\n357\t- Integration follows existing Dashboard conventions: navigation section,\n358\t  per-component feature flag via OdhDashboardConfig, reading state through the\n359\t  Kubernetes API surface the platform evaluation capability exposes.\n360\t- No new data plane: the Dashboard reads and displays; it owns no evaluation\n361\t  state.\n362\t\n363\t## Acceptance Criteria\n364\t\n365\t- Users can view the evaluation runs of a data science project with each run's\n366\t  current status, updating as runs progress.\n367\t- Users can open a run and see its per-benchmark scores.\n368\t- Users can select two model versions and see their evaluation results compared\n369\t  side by side.\n370\t- The views render correctly for a completed run and reflect an in-progress\n371\t  run's status.\n372\t- The integration is behind a Dashboard feature flag (OdhDashboardConfig) and\n373\t  disabled by default until the capability is enabled.\n374\t\n375\t## HLR Traceability\n376\t\n377\tHLR-5 (P1): the Dashboard shows evaluation runs, their status, and\n378\tper-benchmark scores, and allows side-by-side comparison of two model versions.\n379\t\n380\t## Risks / Notes\n381\t\n382\t- The data-source integration (run CR vs. service API) depends on the execution\n383\t  engine decision; the user-facing scope is identical either way. Start after\n384\t  that decision resolves.\n385\t===== artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9002-E008.md =====\n386\t---\n387\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9002-E008\n388\ttitle: 'Data Science Pipelines: evaluation step for promotion-gating flows'\n389\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9002\n390\tcomponent: Data Science Pipelines\n391\tteam: RHOAI Pipelines\n392\ttype: Implementation\n393\tpriority: P1\n394\tdependencies:\n395\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n396\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E006\n397\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n398\tgate_failure_impact:\n399\t  action: rewrite\n400\t  fallback_approach: The pipeline step shape and gating behavior are unchanged by\n401\t    the execution engine decision, but the launch invocation is rewritten to whichever\n402\t    surface the decision selects.\n403\tai_signals:\n404\t  change_specificity: 0\n405\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n406\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n407\t  existing_foundation: 1\n408\t  open_questions: -1\n409\t  external_dependency: 1\n410\t  human_process_gates: 1\n411\t  repo_access: 1\n412\t  architecture_claims: 1\n413\timplementation_type: null\n414\tinvestigation_signals: null\n415\tai_implementability: High\n416\tai_implementability_score: 6\n417\tjira_key: null\n418\tbranch: null\n419\t---\n420\t# Data Science Pipelines: Evaluation step for promotion-gating flows\n421\t\n422\t## Description\n423\t\n424\tMake evaluation embeddable as a step in a Data Science Pipeline, so promotion\n425\tflows can gate on evaluation scores. A pipeline author adds an evaluation step\n426\tthat runs a benchmark suite against a served model; downstream steps gate on\n427\tthe resulting scores. Humans make the promotion decision — the pipeline gates,\n428\tit does not promote (automatic promotion is explicitly out of scope for this\n429\tstrategy).\n430\t\n431\t## Scope\n432\t\n433\t- A reusable pipeline step (KFP v2 component) that launches an evaluation of a\n434\t  model served on the platform against a selected benchmark suite, standard or\n435\t  custom.\n436\t- Support for gating downstream pipeline steps on evaluation scores, e.g.\n437\t  threshold checks against the persisted results linked to the model version.\n438\t- The step surfaces the evaluation's outcome (scores, pass/fail against\n439\t  thresholds) to the pipeline run for branching.\n440\t- A documented example of a promotion-gating flow using the step.\n441\t\n442\t## Acceptance Criteria\n443\t\n444\t- A Data Science Pipeline can include an evaluation step that runs against a\n445\t  served model and completes with the evaluation's status.\n446\t- Downstream steps can gate on the evaluation's scores (pass/fail on a\n447\t  configurable threshold) using the persisted results.\n448\t- The step runs against any benchmark suite exposed by the evaluation launch\n449\t  surface at the time of invocation: the standard suites at launch, and custom\n450\t  evaluation tasks once the deferred custom-task capability lands — with no\n451\t  change to the step's interface.\n452\t- A worked example of a promotion-gating pipeline is provided, with the\n453\t  promotion decision left to a human.\n454\t\n455\t## HLR Traceability\n456\t\n457\tHLR-6 (P1): evaluations can be embedded as a step in a Data Science Pipeline so\n458\tpromotion flows can gate on scores.\n459\t\n460\t## Risks / Notes\n461\t\n462\t- The step's launch invocation (API or CR) depends on the execution engine\n463\t  decision; the component shape and gating behavior are identical either way.\n464\t  Start after that decision resolves.\n465\t- Custom evaluation task support is delivered by the deferred custom-task work\n466\t  in the selected execution-engine branch (a P1 follow-on, deferrable\n467\t  independently of this epic). This epic's step invokes whatever suite the\n468\t  launch surface exposes, so custom-task support is validated when that\n469\t  capability lands rather than gated here.\n470\t===== artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9002-E009.md =====\n471\t---\n472\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9002-E009\n473\ttitle: 'Documentation: end-to-end model evaluation workflow'\n474\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9002\n475\tcomponent: Documentation\n476\tteam: RHOAI Documentation\n477\ttype: Implementation\n478\tpriority: P0\n479\timplementation_type: docs-authoring\n480\tdependencies:\n481\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E006\n482\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E007\n483\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E008\n484\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E003\n485\tai_signals:\n486\t  change_specificity: 0\n487\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n488\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n489\t  existing_foundation: 1\n490\t  open_questions: -1\n491\t  external_dependency: 1\n492\t  human_process_gates: -1\n493\t  repo_access: 1\n494\t  architecture_claims: 1\n495\tinvestigation_signals: null\n496\tai_implementability: High\n497\tai_implementability_score: 3\n498\tjira_key: null\n499\tbranch: null\n500\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-9002-E003\n501\tgate_failure_impact:\n502\t  action: rewrite\n503\t  fallback_approach: The benchmark licensing classification determines the documentation's\n504\t    suite topic — which standard suites are bundled out of the box and which the customer\n505\t    must fetch. If the classification changes after drafting, the suite-delivery topic\n506\t    is rewritten to match it; the remaining documentation topics are unaffected.\n507\t---\n508\t# Documentation: End-to-end model evaluation workflow\n509\t\n510\t## Description\n511\t\n512\tAuthor product documentation for the model evaluation capability, covering the\n513\tworkflow end to end — from launching an evaluation of a served model to\n514\tcomparing candidates and gating promotion flows — including custom task\n515\tauthoring. Documentation is authored through the Red Hat docs content process.\n516\t\n517\t## Scope\n518\t\n519\tContent outline:\n520\t\n521\t- Evaluation concepts: benchmark suites, evaluation runs, results, and their\n522\t  linkage to registered model versions.\n523\t- Launching an evaluation of a model served on the platform (KServe endpoint or\n524\t  Llama Stack distribution) against a selected benchmark suite.\n525\t- Out-of-the-box standard suites vs. customer-fetched datasets, reflecting the\n526\t  benchmark licensing classification.\n527\t- Authoring custom evaluation tasks: bringing your own datasets and metrics and\n528\t  running them through the same flow.\n529\t- Viewing runs, status, and per-benchmark scores in the Dashboard; comparing\n530\t  two model versions side by side.\n531\t- Embedding evaluation in Data Science Pipelines to gate promotion flows.\n532\t- Interpreting cost/duration metadata (tokens, wall-clock, GPU time) for\n533\t  capacity planning.\n534\t\n535\t## Acceptance Criteria\n536\t\n537\t- The documentation covers the evaluation workflow end to end, including custom\n538\t  task authoring, with each topic above addressed.\n539\t- The content is technically reviewed against the implemented capability and\n540\t  validated against the platform architecture context (component names,\n541\t  interfaces, and maturity claims match reality).\n542\t- The topics are published in the RHOAI product documentation set.\n543\t\n544\t## HLR Traceability\n545\t\n546\tHLR-9 (P2): product documentation covers the evaluation workflow end to end,\n547\tincluding custom task authoring. Epic priority is P0 per the docs-authoring\n548\texception (strategy frontmatter priority: Critical → P0).\n549\t===== artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-A-E004.md =====\n550\t---\n551\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-A-E004\n552\ttitle: 'TrustyAI: extend LMEvalJob into the platform evaluation execution path'\n553\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9002\n554\tcomponent: TrustyAI\n555\tteam: TrustyAI\n556\ttype: Implementation\n557\tpriority: P0\n558\tbranch: A\n559\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n560\tdependencies:\n561\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n562\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E002\n563\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E003\n564\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E006\n565\tgate_failure_impact:\n566\t  action: rewrite\n567\t  fallback_approach: If the execution engine investigation recommends a new evaluation\n568\t    service instead of extending LMEvalJob, this epic is replaced by the branch-B\n569\t    service implementation.\n570\tai_signals:\n571\t  change_specificity: 1\n572\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n573\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n574\t  existing_foundation: 1\n575\t  open_questions: -1\n576\t  external_dependency: 1\n577\t  human_process_gates: 0\n578\t  repo_access: 1\n579\t  architecture_claims: 1\n580\timplementation_type: null\n581\tinvestigation_signals: null\n582\tai_implementability: High\n583\tai_implementability_score: 6\n584\tjira_key: null\n585\t---\n586\t# TrustyAI: Extend LMEvalJob into the platform evaluation execution path\n587\t\n588\t## Description\n589\t\n590\tBranch outcome A of the execution engine investigation: build on TrustyAI's\n591\tLMEvalJob (Tech Preview, wrapping lm-evaluation-harness) as the single\n592\texecution path for platform model evaluation. Users launch evaluations of\n593\tmodels served on the platform (KServe endpoints or Llama Stack distributions)\n594\tagainst standard benchmark suites; results are emitted in the canonical result\n595\tschema, written through the Model Registry persistence contract and linked to\n596\tthe evaluated model's registered version, and each run records cost/duration\n597\tmetadata. Custom evaluation tasks (user-defined datasets and metrics) are\n598\tdelivered by a follow-on P1 epic in this branch, deferred independently of\n599\tthis P0 core. This epic\n600\tapplies if the investigation recommends extending LMEvalJob; if it recommends\n601\ta new evaluation service instead, this epic is replaced by the alternative\n602\tbranch's service implementation.\n603\t\n604\t## Scope\n605\t\n606\t- Extend the LMEvalJob path so an evaluation can be launched against a model\n607\t  served on a KServe endpoint or a Llama Stack distribution, with a selected\n608\t  benchmark suite.\n609\t- Deliver the out-of-the-box standard suite set (lm-eval task names) fixed by\n610\t  the benchmark licensing classification: suites classified redistributable\n611\t  are bundled for disconnected environments; customer-fetched suites have a\n612\t  documented fetch path.\n613\t- Emit results in the canonical result schema and write them through the Model\n614\t  Registry persistence and linking contract (owned by the Model Registry\n615\t  persistence work), which links them to the evaluated model's registered\n616\t  version; this epic does not implement its own result storage.\n617\t- Record cost/duration metadata on each run: tokens, wall-clock time, and GPU\n618\t  time.\n619\t- Carry the LMEvalJob capability from Tech Preview toward supported status as\n620\t  part of hardening it into the platform execution path.\n621\t\n622\t## Acceptance Criteria\n623\t\n624\t- A user can launch an evaluation of a model served on the platform (KServe\n625\t  endpoint or Llama Stack distribution) against a selected benchmark suite,\n626\t  and observe the run's status while it executes.\n627\t- Standard suites classified as redistributable are available out of the box in\n628\t  a disconnected environment; suites classified customer-fetched can be\n629\t  retrieved by the customer via the documented path.\n630\t- Results are emitted in the canonical result schema, written through the\n631\t  Model Registry persistence contract, and linked to the evaluated model's\n632\t  Model Registry version.\n633\t- Every run records cost/duration metadata: tokens, wall-clock, and GPU time.\n634\t- Existing LMEvalJob usage is not broken by the extension (backward\n635\t  compatibility for the Tech Preview surface).\n636\t- Changed repositories' build pipelines are green.\n637\t\n638\t## HLR Traceability\n639\t\n640\tHLR-1 (P0): launch an evaluation of a served model against a selected suite.\n641\tHLR-2 (P0): standard benchmark suites available out of the box. HLR-8 (P2):\n642\tcost/duration metadata per run — bundled with the P0 work because it falls out\n643\tof the same run record. HLR-4 (P1, custom evaluation tasks) is covered by the\n644\tfollow-on custom-task epic in this branch, split out so it can be deferred\n645\tindependently of the P0 core.\n646\t\n647\t## Risks / Notes\n648\t\n649\t- LMEvalJob is Tech Preview; hardening it into the supported execution path is\n650\t  part of this epic's cost, and the investigation's recommendation must have\n651\t  weighed it.\n652\t- The single-gate schema records only the execution-engine existence gate;\n653\t  this epic's suite-delivery scope is also materially conditioned on the\n654\t  benchmark licensing classification (redistributable vs. customer-fetched)\n655\t  and on the canonical result schema from the upstream investigations, both\n656\t  carried as plain build-against dependencies.\n657\t===== artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-A-E005.md =====\n658\t---\n659\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-A-E005\n660\ttitle: 'Llama Stack: eval provider backed by the TrustyAI execution path'\n661\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9002\n662\tcomponent: Llama Stack\n663\tteam: RHOAI GenAI\n664\ttype: Implementation\n665\tpriority: P1\n666\tbranch: A\n667\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n668\tdependencies:\n669\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n670\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-A-E004\n671\tgate_failure_impact:\n672\t  action: rewrite\n673\t  fallback_approach: If the execution engine investigation selects the new evaluation\n674\t    service, the provider is wired to that service instead (branch B); the plugin\n675\t    work is rewritten, not dropped.\n676\tai_signals:\n677\t  change_specificity: 0\n678\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n679\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n680\t  existing_foundation: 1\n681\t  open_questions: -1\n682\t  external_dependency: -1\n683\t  human_process_gates: 0\n684\t  repo_access: 1\n685\t  architecture_claims: 1\n686\timplementation_type: null\n687\tinvestigation_signals: null\n688\tai_implementability: High\n689\tai_implementability_score: 3\n690\tjira_key: null\n691\t---\n692\t# Llama Stack: Eval provider backed by the TrustyAI execution path\n693\t\n694\t## Description\n695\t\n696\tBranch outcome A of the execution engine investigation: wire a production eval\n697\tprovider into the platform's Llama Stack distribution, backed by the TrustyAI\n698\tLMEvalJob execution path, so agents and apps built on Llama Stack get the same\n699\tevaluation results as the platform evaluation flow. Today the eval provider API\n700\texists upstream but no production eval provider is wired into the platform\n701\tdistribution.\n702\t\n703\t## Scope\n704\t\n705\t- Implement and configure the Llama Stack eval provider plugin for the platform\n706\t  distribution, backed by the platform evaluation service surface exposed by\n707\t  the TrustyAI execution path.\n708\t- Map the eval provider API onto platform evaluation launches and results:\n709\t  benchmark suite selection maps to the platform's suite set, and returned\n710\t  results use the canonical result schema.\n711\t- Make the provider available in LlamaStackDistribution deployments without\n712\t  custom user wiring.\n713\t\n714\t## Acceptance Criteria\n715\t\n716\t- A Llama Stack app or agent using the eval provider API receives results\n717\t  consistent with the platform evaluation flow for the same model and suite.\n718\t- The provider is available in platform Llama Stack distributions by default,\n719\t  requiring no custom user configuration.\n720\t- Provider-level errors map sensibly to platform evaluation failures (run\n721\t  failures surface as provider errors, not silent empty results).\n722\t\n723\t## HLR Traceability\n724\t\n725\tHLR-7 (P1): Llama Stack's eval provider API is backed by the platform\n726\tevaluation service, so agents/apps built on Llama Stack get the same results.\n727\t\n728\t## Risks / Notes\n729\t\n730\t- If the provider cannot be carried entirely in the midstream distribution,\n731\t  coordination with upstream Llama Stack (meta-llama/llama-stack) may be\n732\t  needed; treat that as a risk to escalate, not a reason to redesign the\n733\t  platform-side wiring.\n734\t===== artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-A-E006.md =====\n735\t---\n736\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-A-E006\n737\ttitle: 'TrustyAI: custom evaluation tasks on the LMEvalJob execution path'\n738\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9002\n739\tcomponent: TrustyAI\n740\tteam: TrustyAI\n741\ttype: Implementation\n742\tpriority: P1\n743\tbranch: A\n744\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n745\tdependencies:\n746\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n747\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-A-E004\n748\tgate_failure_impact:\n749\t  action: rewrite\n750\t  fallback_approach: If the execution engine investigation recommends a new evaluation\n751\t    service instead of extending LMEvalJob, this epic is replaced by the branch-B\n752\t    custom-task epic built on that service.\n753\tai_signals:\n754\t  change_specificity: 1\n755\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n756\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n757\t  existing_foundation: 1\n758\t  open_questions: -1\n759\t  external_dependency: 1\n760\t  human_process_gates: 0\n761\t  repo_access: 1\n762\t  architecture_claims: 1\n763\timplementation_type: null\n764\tinvestigation_signals: null\n765\tai_implementability: High\n766\tai_implementability_score: 6\n767\tjira_key: null\n768\t---\n769\t# TrustyAI: Custom evaluation tasks on the LMEvalJob execution path\n770\t\n771\t## Description\n772\t\n773\tBranch outcome A of the execution engine investigation, follow-on to the\n774\texecution-path epic: users can define custom evaluation tasks — their own\n775\tdatasets and metrics — and run them through the same platform evaluation flow\n776\tbuilt on the extended LMEvalJob capability. Custom task support is a distinct,\n777\tdeferrable feature (P1): it is split from the P0 launch/execution core so the\n778\tstandard-suite capability can ship without it, and this epic extends the\n779\texisting execution surface via a dependency rather than rebuilding it. This\n780\tepic applies if the investigation recommends extending LMEvalJob; if it\n781\trecommends a new evaluation service instead, this epic is replaced by the\n782\talternative branch's custom-task epic.\n783\t\n784\t## Scope\n785\t\n786\t- Support user-defined evaluation tasks — custom datasets and custom metrics —\n787\t  registered through lm-evaluation-harness's custom-task mechanism and launched\n788\t  through the same flow as the out-of-the-box standard suites.\n789\t- Accept user-supplied task definitions (dataset and metric) without changes to\n790\t  the execution path's deployment or serving configuration.\n791\t- Emit custom-task results in the canonical result schema, written through the\n792\t  Model Registry persistence and linking contract so they are linked to the\n793\t  evaluated model's registered version alongside standard-suite results.\n794\t- Record cost/duration metadata (tokens, wall-clock, GPU time) on custom-task\n795\t  runs, using the same run record as standard-suite runs.\n796\t\n797\t## Acceptance Criteria\n798\t\n799\t- A user can define a custom evaluation task (own dataset, own metric) and\n800\t  launch it against a model served on the platform through the same flow used\n801\t  for standard suites.\n802\t- A custom-task run's status is observable while it executes, like a\n803\t  standard-suite run.\n804\t- Custom-task results are persisted in the canonical result schema, linked to\n805\t  the evaluated model's Model Registry version, with cost/duration metadata\n806\t  recorded.\n807\t- Defining a custom task requires no change to the execution path's deployment\n808\t  or serving configuration.\n809\t- Changed repositories' build pipelines are green.\n810\t\n811\t## HLR Traceability\n812\t\n813\tHLR-4 (P1): users can define custom evaluation tasks (own datasets and\n814\tmetrics) and run them through the same flow. Split from the P0 execution epic\n815\tso it can be deferred independently of the standard-suite launch/execution\n816\tcore; it extends the execution surface that epic delivers.\n817\t\n818\t## Risks / Notes\n819\t\n820\t- User-supplied datasets and metrics may need validation/sandboxing\n821\t  considerations on the execution path; carried inside this epic.\n822\t- The single-gate schema records only the execution-engine existence gate;\n823\t  this epic also builds against the canonical result schema and the Model\n824\t  Registry persistence contract produced by the upstream investigations,\n825\t  carried as its dependency chain.\n826\t===== artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E004.md =====\n827\t---\n828\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E004\n829\ttitle: 'TrustyAI: new platform evaluation service'\n830\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9002\n831\tcomponent: TrustyAI\n832\tteam: TrustyAI\n833\ttype: Implementation\n834\tpriority: P0\n835\tbranch: B\n836\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n837\tdependencies:\n838\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n839\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E002\n840\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E003\n841\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E006\n842\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E007\n843\tgate_failure_impact:\n844\t  action: rewrite\n845\t  fallback_approach: If the execution engine investigation recommends extending LMEvalJob\n846\t    instead of building a new service, this epic is replaced by the branch-A LMEvalJob\n847\t    extension.\n848\tai_signals:\n849\t  change_specificity: -1\n850\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n851\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n852\t  existing_foundation: -1\n853\t  open_questions: -1\n854\t  external_dependency: 1\n855\t  human_process_gates: 0\n856\t  repo_access: 1\n857\t  architecture_claims: 1\n858\timplementation_type: null\n859\tinvestigation_signals: null\n860\tai_implementability: Medium\n861\tai_implementability_score: 1\n862\tjira_key: null\n863\t---\n864\t# TrustyAI: New platform evaluation service\n865\t\n866\t## Description\n867\t\n868\tBranch outcome B of the execution engine investigation: build a new evaluation\n869\tservice as the platform's execution path for model evaluation. The service\n870\twraps lm-evaluation-harness, executes benchmark runs against models served on\n871\tthe platform (KServe endpoints or Llama Stack distributions), persists results\n872\tin the canonical schema via the Model Registry persistence contract linked to\n873\tModel Registry versions, and exposes a service API suitable as the backend for\n874\tLlama Stack's eval provider API. Custom evaluation tasks (user-defined\n875\tdatasets and metrics) are delivered by a follow-on P1 epic in this branch,\n876\tdeferred independently of this P0 core. This epic applies if the investigation recommends a new service; if it recommends\n877\textending LMEvalJob instead, this epic is replaced by the alternative branch's\n878\tLMEvalJob extension.\n879\t\n880\t## Scope\n881\t\n882\t- Build the evaluation service: execution of lm-eval benchmark runs against\n883\t  models served on KServe endpoints or Llama Stack distributions.\n884\t- Define and implement the launch surface (how an evaluation is started) and\n885\t  run-status observation for consumers.\n886\t- Deliver the out-of-the-box standard suite set fixed by the benchmark\n887\t  licensing classification: redistributable suites bundled for disconnected\n888\t  environments; customer-fetched suites via a documented fetch path.\n889\t- Emit results in the canonical result schema and write them through the Model\n890\t  Registry persistence and linking contract (owned by the Model Registry\n891\t  persistence work), which links them to the evaluated model's registered\n892\t  version; this epic does not implement its own result storage.\n893\t- Record cost/duration metadata per run: tokens, wall-clock, GPU time.\n894\t- Expose a service API suitable as the Llama Stack eval provider backend.\n895\t- Deliver the service in the delivery repository fixed by this branch's\n896\t  repository-provisioning work (a dedicated midstream repository, or an\n897\t  existing TrustyAI midstream repository if that is the recorded decision).\n898\t\n899\t## Acceptance Criteria\n900\t\n901\t- A user can launch an evaluation of a model served on the platform (KServe\n902\t  endpoint or Llama Stack distribution) against a selected benchmark suite,\n903\t  and observe the run's status while it executes.\n904\t- Standard suites classified as redistributable are available out of the box in\n905\t  a disconnected environment; customer-fetched suites have a documented fetch\n906\t  path.\n907\t- Results are emitted in the canonical result schema, written through the\n908\t  Model Registry persistence contract, and linked to the evaluated model's\n909\t  Model Registry version.\n910\t- Every run records cost/duration metadata: tokens, wall-clock, GPU time.\n911\t- The service API is sufficient to back Llama Stack's eval provider API.\n912\t- The service's delivery repository has a green build pipeline for the\n913\t  service's image builds.\n914\t\n915\t## HLR Traceability\n916\t\n917\tHLR-1 (P0): launch an evaluation of a served model against a selected suite.\n918\tHLR-2 (P0): standard benchmark suites available out of the box. HLR-8 (P2):\n919\tcost/duration metadata per run — bundled with the P0 work because it falls out\n920\tof the same run record. HLR-4 (P1, custom evaluation tasks) is covered by the\n921\tfollow-on custom-task epic in this branch, split out so it can be deferred\n922\tindependently of the P0 core.\n923\t\n924\t## Risks / Notes\n925\t\n926\t- Greenfield service: higher implementation and maintenance risk than\n927\t  extending the existing LMEvalJob path; the investigation's recommendation\n928\t  must have weighed this explicitly.\n929\t- The service is mapped to the TrustyAI component (the platform's model\n930\t  quality service family); there is no separate canonical component for an\n931\t  evaluation service.\n932\t- The single-gate schema records only the execution-engine existence gate;\n933\t  this epic's suite-delivery scope is also materially conditioned on the\n934\t  benchmark licensing classification (redistributable vs. customer-fetched)\n935\t  and on the canonical result schema from the upstream investigations, both\n936\t  carried as plain build-against dependencies.\n937\t- The delivery-vehicle decision (dedicated repository vs. existing TrustyAI\n938\t  midstream repositories) and the contingent repository provisioning and\n939\t  build-pipeline onboarding are split into this branch's repository-provisioning\n940\t  epic; this epic is serial with it (the service code lands in the repository\n941\t  it fixes) so that the provisioning contingency, when it triggers, does not\n942\t  oversize the service delivery.\n943\t===== artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E005.md =====\n944\t---\n945\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E005\n946\ttitle: 'Llama Stack: eval provider backed by the new evaluation service'\n947\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9002\n948\tcomponent: Llama Stack\n949\tteam: RHOAI GenAI\n950\ttype: Implementation\n951\tpriority: P1\n952\tbranch: B\n953\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n954\tdependencies:\n955\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n956\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E004\n957\tgate_failure_impact:\n958\t  action: rewrite\n959\t  fallback_approach: If the execution engine investigation selects the LMEvalJob path,\n960\t    the provider is wired to that path instead (branch A); the plugin work is rewritten,\n961\t    not dropped.\n962\tai_signals:\n963\t  change_specificity: 0\n964\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n965\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n966\t  existing_foundation: 1\n967\t  open_questions: -1\n968\t  external_dependency: -1\n969\t  human_process_gates: 0\n970\t  repo_access: 1\n971\t  architecture_claims: 1\n972\timplementation_type: null\n973\tinvestigation_signals: null\n974\tai_implementability: High\n975\tai_implementability_score: 3\n976\tjira_key: null\n977\t---\n978\t# Llama Stack: Eval provider backed by the new evaluation service\n979\t\n980\t## Description\n981\t\n982\tBranch outcome B of the execution engine investigation: wire a production eval\n983\tprovider into the platform's Llama Stack distribution, backed by the new\n984\tplatform evaluation service, so agents and apps built on Llama Stack get the\n985\tsame evaluation results as the platform evaluation flow. Today the eval\n986\tprovider API exists upstream but no production eval provider is wired into the\n987\tplatform distribution.\n988\t\n989\t## Scope\n990\t\n991\t- Implement and configure the Llama Stack eval provider plugin for the platform\n992\t  distribution, backed by the new evaluation service's API.\n993\t- Map the eval provider API onto the service's evaluation launches and\n994\t  results: benchmark suite selection maps to the platform's suite set, and\n995\t  returned results use the canonical result schema.\n996\t- Make the provider available in LlamaStackDistribution deployments without\n997\t  custom user wiring.\n998\t\n999\t## Acceptance Criteria\n1000\t\n1001\t- A Llama Stack app or agent using the eval provider API receives results\n1002\t  consistent with the platform evaluation flow for the same model and suite.\n1003\t- The provider is available in platform Llama Stack distributions by default,\n1004\t  requiring no custom user configuration.\n1005\t- Provider-level errors map sensibly to evaluation service failures (run\n1006\t  failures surface as provider errors, not silent empty results).\n1007\t\n1008\t## HLR Traceability\n1009\t\n1010\tHLR-7 (P1): Llama Stack's eval provider API is backed by the platform\n1011\tevaluation service, so agents/apps built on Llama Stack get the same results.\n1012\t\n1013\t## Risks / Notes\n1014\t\n1015\t- If the provider cannot be carried entirely in the midstream distribution,\n1016\t  coordination with upstream Llama Stack (meta-llama/llama-stack) may be\n1017\t  needed; treat that as a risk to escalate, not a reason to redesign the\n1018\t  platform-side wiring.\n1019\t===== artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E006.md =====\n1020\t---\n1021\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E006\n1022\ttitle: 'TrustyAI: custom evaluation tasks on the new evaluation service'\n1023\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9002\n1024\tcomponent: TrustyAI\n1025\tteam: TrustyAI\n1026\ttype: Implementation\n1027\tpriority: P1\n1028\tbranch: B\n1029\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n1030\tdependencies:\n1031\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n1032\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E004\n1033\tgate_failure_impact:\n1034\t  action: rewrite\n1035\t  fallback_approach: If the execution engine investigation recommends extending LMEvalJob\n1036\t    instead of building a new evaluation service, this epic is replaced by the branch-A\n1037\t    custom-task epic built on the LMEvalJob execution path.\n1038\tai_signals:\n1039\t  change_specificity: 1\n1040\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n1041\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n1042\t  existing_foundation: 0\n1043\t  open_questions: -1\n1044\t  external_dependency: 1\n1045\t  human_process_gates: 0\n1046\t  repo_access: 1\n1047\t  architecture_claims: 1\n1048\timplementation_type: null\n1049\tinvestigation_signals: null\n1050\tai_implementability: High\n1051\tai_implementability_score: 5\n1052\tjira_key: null\n1053\t---\n1054\t# TrustyAI: Custom evaluation tasks on the new evaluation service\n1055\t\n1056\t## Description\n1057\t\n1058\tBranch outcome B of the execution engine investigation, follow-on to the\n1059\tevaluation service epic: users can define custom evaluation tasks — their own\n1060\tdatasets and metrics — and run them through the same platform evaluation flow\n1061\tserved by the new evaluation service. Custom task support is a distinct,\n1062\tdeferrable feature (P1): it is split from the P0 service delivery so the\n1063\tstandard-suite capability can ship without it, and this epic extends the\n1064\tservice's existing launch surface via a dependency rather than rebuilding it.\n1065\tThis epic applies if the investigation recommends a new evaluation service; if\n1066\tit recommends extending LMEvalJob instead, this epic is replaced by the\n1067\talternative branch's custom-task epic.\n1068\t\n1069\t## Scope\n1070\t\n1071\t- Support user-defined evaluation tasks — custom datasets and custom metrics —\n1072\t  accepted by the evaluation service and executed through the same flow as the\n1073\t  out-of-the-box standard suites.\n1074\t- Accept user-supplied task definitions (dataset and metric) without changes\n1075\t  to the service's deployment or serving configuration.\n1076\t- Emit custom-task results in the canonical result schema, written through the\n1077\t  Model Registry persistence and linking contract so they are linked to the\n1078\t  evaluated model's registered version alongside standard-suite results.\n1079\t- Record cost/duration metadata (tokens, wall-clock, GPU time) on custom-task\n1080\t  runs, using the same run record as standard-suite runs.\n1081\t\n1082\t## Acceptance Criteria\n1083\t\n1084\t- A user can define a custom evaluation task (own dataset, own metric) and\n1085\t  launch it against a model served on the platform through the same flow used\n1086\t  for standard suites.\n1087\t- A custom-task run's status is observable while it executes, like a\n1088\t  standard-suite run.\n1089\t- Custom-task results are persisted in the canonical result schema, linked to\n1090\t  the evaluated model's Model Registry version, with cost/duration metadata\n1091\t  recorded.\n1092\t- Defining a custom task requires no change to the service's deployment or\n1093\t  serving configuration.\n1094\t- Changed repositories' build pipelines are green.\n1095\t\n1096\t## HLR Traceability\n1097\t\n1098\tHLR-4 (P1): users can define custom evaluation tasks (own datasets and\n1099\tmetrics) and run them through the same flow. Split from the P0 service epic\n1100\tso it can be deferred independently of the standard-suite launch/execution\n1101\tcore; it extends the service surface that epic delivers.\n1102\t\n1103\t## Risks / Notes\n1104\t\n1105\t- The foundation is the newly delivered evaluation service, which is less\n1106\t  proven in production than the LMEvalJob path; custom-task support must track\n1107\t  the service's API as it stabilizes.\n1108\t- User-supplied datasets and metrics may need validation/sandboxing\n1109\t  considerations in the service; carried inside this epic.\n1110\t- The single-gate schema records only the execution-engine existence gate;\n1111\t  this epic also builds against the canonical result schema and the Model\n1112\t  Registry persistence contract produced by the upstream investigations,\n1113\t  carried as its dependency chain.\n1114\t===== artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E007.md =====\n1115\t---\n1116\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E007\n1117\ttitle: 'TrustyAI: conditional repository provisioning for the evaluation service'\n1118\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9002\n1119\tcomponent: TrustyAI\n1120\tteam: TrustyAI\n1121\ttype: Implementation\n1122\tpriority: P0\n1123\timplementation_type: repo-onboarding\n1124\tdependencies:\n1125\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n1126\tbranch: B\n1127\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n1128\tgate_failure_impact:\n1129\t  action: remove\n1130\t  fallback_approach: Under the branch-A outcome (extend LMEvalJob in the existing\n1131\t    TrustyAI midstream repositories) this epic is removed — no new repository is needed\n1132\t    for that path.\n1133\tai_signals:\n1134\t  change_specificity: 1\n1135\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n1136\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n1137\t  existing_foundation: 1\n1138\t  open_questions: 1\n1139\t  external_dependency: 1\n1140\t  human_process_gates: 0\n1141\t  repo_access: 1\n1142\t  architecture_claims: 0\n1143\tinvestigation_signals: null\n1144\tai_implementability: High\n1145\tai_implementability_score: 7\n1146\tjira_key: null\n1147\t---\n1148\t# TrustyAI: Conditional repository provisioning for the evaluation service\n1149\t\n1150\t## Description\n1151\t\n1152\tBranch B contingency epic for the delivery vehicle of the new evaluation\n1153\tservice: record whether the service ships in a dedicated midstream repository\n1154\tunder opendatahub-io or within the existing TrustyAI midstream repositories,\n1155\tand, if a dedicated repository is chosen, provision it and onboard its build\n1156\tpipeline. This work was split out of the branch-B service-delivery epic so\n1157\tthat the provisioning contingency, when it triggers, does not oversize the\n1158\tservice epic beyond a single sprint. If the execution engine investigation\n1159\trecommends extending LMEvalJob instead of building a new service, this epic\n1160\tis removed along with the rest of branch B — the LMEvalJob path lives in an\n1161\texisting, onboarded repository.\n1162\t\n1163\t## Scope\n1164\t\n1165\t- Record the delivery-vehicle decision for the new evaluation service — a\n1166\t  dedicated midstream repository under opendatahub-io, or delivery within the\n1167\t  existing TrustyAI midstream repositories — with its rationale, before\n1168\t  service implementation starts.\n1169\t- If a dedicated repository is chosen: create the midstream repository with\n1170\t  the standard scaffolding (ownership, CI triggers, branch protection).\n1171\t- If a dedicated repository is chosen: onboard the repository to the build\n1172\t  pipeline so it produces green builds for the service's container image.\n1173\t- If an existing repository is chosen: no repository is provisioned; the epic\n1174\t  closes on the recorded decision.\n1175\t\n1176\t## Acceptance Criteria\n1177\t\n1178\t- The delivery-vehicle decision is recorded with its rationale before the\n1179\t  branch-B service implementation starts.\n1180\t- If a dedicated repository is chosen: the repository exists under\n1181\t  opendatahub-io with the standard scaffolding, and its build pipeline\n1182\t  produces a green build.\n1183\t- If an existing repository is chosen: no repository is provisioned and the\n1184\t  decision record closes this epic.\n1185\t\n1186\t## HLR Traceability\n1187\t\n1188\tSupports HLR-1 and HLR-2 (P0) in branch B by fixing the delivery vehicle for\n1189\tthe execution service. Not a strategy HLR of its own — it is the\n1190\trepository-provisioning contingency the branch-B service epic is serial with.\n1191\t\n1192\t## Risks / Notes\n1193\t\n1194\t- Conditional by design: the provisioning scope executes only if the\n1195\t  delivery-vehicle decision selects a dedicated repository; otherwise the\n1196\t  epic closes on the decision record.\n1197\t- Serial with the branch-B service epic (the service code lands in the\n1198\t  repository this epic fixes); it does not block work in other repositories.\n1199\t- The build-pipeline onboarding is part of this epic's provisioning scope;\n1200\t  the service epic carries the build-pipeline-green acceptance criterion for\n1201\t  its own image builds.\n1202\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-89bfed359e259699","agent_id":"a24371f175b494457","session_id":"b202a2e5-2f2e-4be7-874a-783267f0136d","uuid":"4b5bd9ea-d3fc-49f4-9955-9e9bfd113b2c","timestamp":"2026-10-22T14:23:44.886Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b18fe89520f3c1ec","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Final review after third revision"}
